**Read:** [Termodinâmica dos Materiais — Cota, 2026](../sources/Termodinamica-dos-Materiais-Cota.lingua#r_05ES8F38ASNQEQMZ0HY6709Z0Z), the whole of chapter 8 and its exercises; [Phases, diffusion and solidification — Cambridge DoITPoMS](../sources/Phases-and-Diffusion-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_4HHCB32507KCQ5FPHGT4JD5GTB), Phase Diagrams and Solidification; [Fundamentals of Materials Science — MIT 3.012](../sources/Fundamentals-of-Materials-Science-MIT-3-012.lingua#r_1JE4A3P3KKM0FZXERWFATXCS1Z), thermodynamics lectures 15–20.

Read axes, composition basis, pressure and permitted phases before following a temperature path. A tie line supplies coexisting phase compositions; the overall composition determines their fractions. For a nonreacting bulk equilibrium system with C independent components and P phases, F=C−P+2; fixing pressure removes one degree of freedom. Additional constraints require care.

Distinguish isomorphous, eutectic, peritectic, eutectoid, compound and miscibility-gap behavior by actual phase relationships. A eutectic microconstituent contains multiple phases. An equilibrium diagram alone does not predict grain size, lamellar spacing, retained metastable phases or segregation after finite-rate cooling.

**Work:** Follow three compositions through a documented binary diagram, deriving phase amounts at selected temperatures. Distinguish primary solid from the later two-phase microconstituent. Explain an apparent phase-rule violation by checking assumptions before changing the rule.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
